            {
              "id": "773672d0-38c5-30a8-9bb5-f44e05dd2fad",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment block. The problem is that this function is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are thread-safe, such as `getenv_s` or `getenv_r`. Also, always validate and sanitize the input before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"GGML_VK_SUBALLOCATION_BLOCK_SIZE\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"GGML_VK_SUBALLOCATION_BLOCK_SIZE doesn't exist!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nvalue = (char*)malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\nif (!value)\n{\n    printf(\"Failed to allocate memory!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"GGML_VK_SUBALLOCATION_BLOCK_SIZE\");\n\nGGML_VK_SUBALLOCATION_BLOCK_SIZE = value;\nfree(value);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for `getenv_s`, `malloc`, `free`, and `exit` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
