            {
              "id": "e3a18d61-98b9-3a38-8b1f-a720f2abd822",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source are both pointers, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory areas. The use of a standard memory copy function in such a scenario can lead to unpredictable results, including data corruption or crashes. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to ensure the source is not null, which further increases the risk of runtime errors. The absence of explicit bounds checking or capacity tracking for the destination buffer also contributes to the severity of this issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions. In C, `memmove` is designed for this purpose. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(dst_ptr, src0_ptr, type_size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy is performed correctly even if the source and destination overlap. Additionally, consider adding explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the operation to avoid null pointer dereference issues.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
