            {
              "id": "d613710f-7937-333f-b2c9-9af88852d3d1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen` is used twice to calculate the length of the same string. This can lead to performance issues as the same operation is performed twice. Moreover, if the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` can lead to buffer over-read vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strlen` function in a way that it gets called multiple times for the same string. Instead, you can store the length of the string in a variable and use this variable whenever needed. This will not only improve the performance of your code but also prevent potential buffer over-read vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strlen(buf);\nsnprintf(buf + len, sizeof(buf) - len, \", %5\" PRId64, t->ne[i]);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for the `snprintf` function.\n- `string.h`: for the `strlen` function.\n- `inttypes.h`: for the `PRId64` macro.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021",
                  "d613710f-7937-333f-b2c9-9af88852d3d1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fc328d27-d011-3afd-81dc-7d36548f39ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen` is used twice to calculate the length of the same string. This can lead to performance issues as the same operation is performed twice. Moreover, if the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` can lead to buffer over-read vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strlen` function in a way that it gets called multiple times for the same string. Instead, you can store the length of the string in a variable and use this variable whenever needed. This will not only improve the performance of your code but also prevent potential buffer over-read vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strlen(buf);\nsnprintf(buf + len, sizeof(buf) - len, \", %5\" PRId64, t->ne[i]);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for the `snprintf` function.\n- `string.h`: for the `strlen` function.\n- `inttypes.h`: for the `PRId64` macro.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021",
                  "fc328d27-d011-3afd-81dc-7d36548f39ca"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eb107274-7ef9-3ec4-840b-84fd19a4956f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen` is used twice to calculate the length of the same string. This can lead to a race condition if the string is modified between the two calls to `strlen`, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `strlen` multiple times for the same string in the same context. Instead, the length of the string should be stored in a variable and this variable should be used. This will ensure that the length of the string remains consistent throughout the context and prevent potential race conditions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strlen(buf);\nsnprintf(buf + len, sizeof(buf) - len, \", %5\" PRId64, ne.at(i));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n- `vector` for `at`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-805: Buffer Access with Incorrect Length Value](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/805.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021",
                  "eb107274-7ef9-3ec4-840b-84fd19a4956f"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94504afc-047b-3d63-a738-0de32393eaf8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen` is used twice to calculate the length of the same string. This can lead to a race condition if the string is modified between the two calls to `strlen`, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `strlen` multiple times for the same string in the same context. Instead, the length of the string should be stored in a variable and this variable should be used. This will ensure that the length of the string remains consistent throughout the context and prevent potential race conditions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strlen(buf);\nsnprintf(buf + len, sizeof(buf) - len, \", %5\" PRId64, ne.at(i));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n- `vector` for `at`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-805: Buffer Access with Incorrect Length Value](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/805.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021",
                  "94504afc-047b-3d63-a738-0de32393eaf8"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "56cba0fe-33ac-378e-a42e-398e7944eb4f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the function `vsnprintf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the ones provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use `std::vsnprintf` instead of `vsnprintf()`. Also, always ensure that the size argument passed to these functions does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid safeFunction(const char* fmt, ...) {\n    std::vector<char> buf(size + 1);\n    va_list ap2;\n    va_start(ap2, fmt);\n    std::vsnprintf(buf.data(), buf.size(), fmt, ap2);\n    va_end(ap2);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: For `std::vsnprintf`.\n- `<cstdarg>`: For `va_list`, `va_start`, and `va_end`.\n- `<vector>`: For `std::vector`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "966f7cd8-26d9-378f-874a-c85899730160",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `vsnprintf()` is used, which is a variadic function that writes formatted output from a variable argument list to a sized buffer. If not used properly, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the \"n\" or \"s\" versions of the same functions, which take an additional size parameter to prevent buffer overflows. Also, always validate and sanitize input data before using it in such functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the given code, the size of the buffer is not specified, which can lead to buffer overflow. A safer way to use `vsnprintf()` would be to specify the size of the buffer. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[1024];\nsize = vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), fmt, ap);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31f6b3d4-68de-3cca-89eb-e692383ef44b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the ones provided by the C++ Standard Library. Also, always ensure that the buffer is large enough to hold all the data that you're trying to put in it, including the null terminating character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you should ensure that the size argument passed to `vsnprintf` is less than or equal to the size of the buffer. Also, you should check the return value of `vsnprintf` to make sure that the function succeeded. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdarg>\n\nvoid safe_printf(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[128];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    int len = vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    if (len < 0 || len >= sizeof(buffer)) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    va_end(args);\n    // Use buffer...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for `vsnprintf`\n- `<cstdarg>`: for `va_list`, `va_start`, and `va_end`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e89a5c6d-84cb-36d5-b01a-97de8fa634bc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, leading to potential security risks such as code execution, data corruption, or denial of service. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`, or `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`. Also, always ensure that the size argument correctly represents the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `vsnprintf` function is used. To fix this vulnerability, you should ensure that the size argument (`len + 1` in this case) does not exceed the size of the destination buffer (`buffer2` in this case). Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (len < sizeof(buffer2)) {\n    vsnprintf(buffer2, len + 1, format, args_copy);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer size is not enough\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for the `vsnprintf` function\n- `stdarg.h`: for the `va_list` type and related functions\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6b0a5e97-c668-3702-8f17-75dc561565dc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer could be null at this point in the code, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination is not likely to be null, as it appears to be a local variable or field. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n```c\nif (gas != NULL && &aux32 != gas) {\n    memcpy(&aux32, gas, sizeof(uint32_t));\n} else if (gas != NULL) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove\n    memmove(&aux32, gas, sizeof(uint32_t));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6ac6ab99-71d3-37d1-8c2d-63b12342bbe2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, and the copy size is 12 bytes, which fits within the destination. However, dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there is a possibility that the source pointer could be null, further increasing the risk. No explicit checks or guards are present to prevent these issues. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(utmp, x[i].scales, 12);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap. Additionally, consider adding checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ff175ffb-1f46-3cb9-abd3-2d514b220972",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination is a fixed-size array, and the source is an identifier, with dataflow analysis indicating that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard ensuring that the source is non-null, which increases the risk of a null pointer dereference. The classification explicitly states 'UB Risk: possible src/dst overlap,' and the verdict is marked as genuine. These factors indicate a real risk of undefined behavior, which can lead to unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, add explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy operation.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```c\nif (q2 != NULL && aux32 != q2) {\n    memcpy(aux32, q2, 2 * sizeof(uint32_t));\n} else if (q2 != NULL) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove\n    memmove(aux32, q2, 2 * sizeof(uint32_t));\n}\n```\n\nThis approach first checks for null pointers and then ensures that if the source and destination may overlap, `memmove` is used instead of `memcpy`.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "17b6e6f0-5dbd-36fc-bf04-767303161e4f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, and the copy size is 12 bytes, which fits within the buffer. However, dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, which would also cause undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(utmp, x[i].scales, 12);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```c\nif (x[i].scales != NULL) {\n    memmove(utmp, x[i].scales, 12);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2c63cc50-3fc3-3a55-a216-6b710270b080",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, but the copy operation uses a hardcoded length of 12 bytes, which matches the size of three 32-bit integers. The analysis also detected, through data flow, that the source and destination may overlap, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null, which could lead to a crash. There are no explicit checks or guards to prevent these issues, and the classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with `memmove`, which is safe for overlapping regions. Also, ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid a potential crash.\n\n```c\nif (x[i].scales != NULL) {\n    memmove(auxs, x[i].scales, 12);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ee2c113c-4f10-3420-83c2-d9a883d8acfe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, the source may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue. Such undefined behavior can lead to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&qh, x[ib].qh, sizeof(qh));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null to avoid undefined behavior. For example:\n\n```c\nif (x[ib].qh != NULL) {\n    memcpy(&qh, x[ib].qh, sizeof(qh));\n}\n```\n\nThis check prevents dereferencing a null pointer, which could otherwise lead to a crash or security issue.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "637aab4e-13d4-3ae0-98c9-e2a42bab03fe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory, and the use of memcpy in such cases is unsafe and can lead to unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, which would also result in undefined behavior. There are no explicit guards or checks in the code to prevent these issues, and the classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. For example, change the code to:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&qh, x[ib].qh, sizeof(qh));\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap. Additionally, if there is any possibility that the source pointer could be null, add a check before the copy:\n\n```c\nif (x[ib].qh != NULL) {\n    memmove(&qh, x[ib].qh, sizeof(qh));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f02f5758-0902-3260-9940-55dfbf2fe2be",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(x, &a, sizeof(ggml_fp16_t))`, if the size of `x` is less than `sizeof(ggml_fp16_t)`, it can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Also, consider using safer functions like `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` which include the size of the destination as a parameter.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code snippet using `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(ggml_fp16_t);\nif(sizeof(x) >= size) {\n    memcpy_s(x, size, &a, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed for the code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1ce3737-8c64-3399-a0fa-838f37d4ee22",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&a, x, sizeof(ggml_fp16_t))`, if the size of `x` is larger than the size of `a`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before the `memcpy` operation.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer functions that include the size of the destination buffer as a parameter, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11), which will not write past the specified buffer size.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nggml_fp16_t a;\nchar x[sizeof(ggml_fp16_t)];\n// Ensure x is initialized\nmemcpy(&a, x, sizeof(a));\n```\n\nIn this version, `x` is guaranteed to be the same size as `a`, preventing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ee8b9042-8f5e-3438-95f0-e3f476a83828",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&res, &tmp, sizeof(ggml_fp16_t))`, if the size of `tmp` is larger than `res`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, system crashes, and unauthorized code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination buffers before calling `memcpy`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy` such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11), which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code snippet using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Ensure res is large enough to hold tmp\nif (sizeof(res) >= sizeof(tmp)) {\n    strncpy(&res, &tmp, sizeof(ggml_fp16_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncpy` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "727b05f7-98e1-33c4-b7cf-7b7a96e1371b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the destination buffer is smaller than the size of the source buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&s, &f, sizeof(uint16_t))`, if the size of `s` is less than `sizeof(uint16_t)`, it can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nuint16_t s;\nuint16_t f;\n\nif(sizeof(s) >= sizeof(f)) {\n    memcpy(&s, &f, sizeof(uint16_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if the size of `s` is greater than or equal to the size of `f` before calling `memcpy`. If it's not, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "59c8b759-7964-3c75-8758-bcc795deefbc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if the size of `ggml_fp16_t` is larger than the size of `tmp`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the data and the size of the buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy` such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` which include additional checks to prevent buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(tmp) >= sizeof(ggml_fp16_t)) {\n    memcpy(&tmp, &h, sizeof(ggml_fp16_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we check if the size of `tmp` is larger or equal to the size of `ggml_fp16_t` before calling `memcpy`. If it's not, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
