            {
              "id": "be669774-4fb8-328b-a9e5-ea54dd8028a8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being used as the source in a memory copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', which further supports this assessment. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, and the code attempts to copy 12 bytes into it. While the size appears to match the buffer's capacity, there is no evidence of checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the copy occurs. The analysis could not resolve the base of the source pointer, so it conservatively assumes that a null pointer dereference is possible. There is no indication of overlapping memory regions, and the destination is not likely to be null, but the lack of null checks for the source is a significant risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. You can add a check before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source pointer is valid before attempting to copy memory. If the pointer can never be null by design, document this assumption clearly in the code and consider adding an assertion for defensive programming:\n\n```cpp\nassert(b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr);\nmemcpy(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n```\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d38c27d-6fa2-31e8-971e-a9431817f8e6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer may be null at the point of the memory copy operation. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which provides a capacity of 16 bytes, and the copy operation requests 12 bytes, which fits within the destination. However, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the copy occurs. If the source pointer is null, this will result in a crash or undefined behavior. The destination buffer is not at risk of being null, as it is a local or field array, but the source pointer's validity cannot be guaranteed based on the available information. No overlap between source and destination is detected, and the copy size is within bounds, but the lack of null checks on the source pointer is a significant risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to verify that `b_ptr[b].scales` is not null before using it in the memcpy operation. This prevents undefined behavior if the source pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_0, b_ptr[b].scales + 24 * sb, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return an error code\n}\n```\n\nIf the code must always guarantee a valid source, consider adding assertions or error handling to catch programming errors early.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
