            {
              "id": "ccd27295-7ba2-375f-89f7-295168ad73b8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null checks before the memory copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which provides a capacity of 16 bytes, and the copy operation requests 12 bytes, so there is no immediate risk of buffer overflow. However, the analysis could not confirm that the source pointer is always valid, and the source may be null at runtime, which would result in a crash or undefined behavior. There is no evidence of overlapping memory regions, and the destination is not a pointer-based member, reducing some risks. The absence of explicit null checks for the source pointer is the primary concern here.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior or crashes. Add an explicit check for the source pointer before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // e.g., log an error, return an error code, or take other appropriate action\n}\n```\n\nIf the source pointer can never be null by design, document this assumption clearly in the code with an assertion:\n\n```cpp\nassert(b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr);\nmemcpy(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
