              "id": "5d5aff96-4392-33c2-bb96-459bae029ad2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being used as the source in the memory copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', which further confirms the presence of a real issue. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which provides a capacity of 16 bytes, and the copy operation requests 12 bytes, so there is no immediate risk of buffer overflow. However, the analysis could not confirm that the source pointer is always valid and non-null, and there are no explicit checks to ensure the source is not null before the copy. This means that if the source pointer is ever null, the program may crash or exhibit undefined behavior. There is no evidence of overlapping memory regions, and the destination is not a pointer-based member, reducing some risks, but the lack of null checks on the source is a significant concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. Add an explicit check for the source pointer before calling the memory copy function:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr_1[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_11, b_ptr_1[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return an error code\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory copy is only performed when the source pointer is valid, preventing crashes or undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
