              "id": "8805a36e-47ec-3b21-8800-38b27e119d4e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer in the memory copy operation may be null. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which provides a capacity of 16 bytes, and the copy operation requests 12 bytes, which fits within the destination. However, there are no checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the copy occurs. The analysis could not resolve the base of the source pointer, so it conservatively assumes it may be null. The absence of overlap between source and destination, and the fact that the destination is not a pointer-based member, reduce some risks, but the potential for a null source pointer remains a critical issue. This can lead to a crash or other undefined behavior if the source is indeed null at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. This can be done by adding a check before the call to the memory copy function. If the source pointer is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the issue, or take corrective action).\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr_1[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_10, b_ptr_1[b].scales + sb * 24, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return from the function\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
