              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null checks for either the source or destination. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', which further supports this assessment. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, and the code copies 12 bytes into it, which matches the buffer size (4 * 4 bytes = 16 bytes), so there is no immediate risk of buffer overflow. However, the main concern is that the source pointer could be null, as the analysis could not resolve the base of the source pointer and conservatively assumes it may be null. There is no evidence of overlap between source and destination, and the destination is not likely to be null. The absence of null pointer checks for the source is the primary risk here.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore calling the memory copy operation, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. Add an explicit check for the source pointer before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr_0[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_00, b_ptr_0[b].scales + 24 * sb, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return an error code\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory copy only occurs if the source pointer is valid, preventing a potential crash or undefined behavior if the source is null.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
