              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in a memory copy operation. The destination and source pointers may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. This can cause unpredictable results with functions like memcpy, which require non-overlapping regions. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the source pointer may also be null, which could lead to a crash. The destination pointer is not likely to be null, but the combination of possible overlap and a potentially null source makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(quant_offset, group->qs, group_quant_size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, add checks to ensure that the source pointer (`group->qs`) is not null before performing the copy:\n\n```cpp\nif (group->qs != NULL) {\n    memmove(quant_offset, group->qs, group_quant_size);\n}\n```\n\nThis approach eliminates the risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory and null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "14ae40e2-28df-3d97-a1f6-353c9779fb31",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the function `std::getenv` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities because it returns a pointer to a string that represents the value of the environment variable. This string can be modified by another process, leading to potential race conditions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` and instead use a safer alternative that does not have the same security risks. If you must use `std::getenv`, ensure that you handle the returned string carefully to avoid race conditions. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, you can use `std::getenv_s`, which is a safer alternative. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, name.c_str());\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    std::cout << \"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\";\n}\nelse\n{\n    char* value = new char[requiredSize];\n    getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, name.c_str());\n    val = value;\n    delete[] value;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ca2ff11e-61e9-3f7f-9354-0014c528f740",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointers, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The code copies 12 bytes into a buffer of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which matches the buffer size (4 * 4 = 16 bytes), so there is no immediate overflow. However, the source pointer may be null, as the analysis could not resolve its base, and there are no explicit null checks before the memory copy. The destination buffer is likely safe as it is a local or field array, not a pointer. There is no evidence of overlapping memory regions, and the destination buffer is not null. The main risk is dereferencing a potentially null source pointer, which can lead to a crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to verify that `b_ptr_0[b].scales` is not null before using it in pointer arithmetic and as the source for `memcpy`. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr_0[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_01, b_ptr_0[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // e.g., log an error, return an error code, or take other appropriate action\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d3af5e66-a591-3541-9785-d24e3b6ea780",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer may be null at the point of the memory copy operation. The verdict for this call site is 'GENUINE', indicating high confidence in the issue. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, which provides a capacity of 16 bytes, and the copy operation requests 12 bytes, which fits within the destination. However, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the copy, and the analysis could not resolve the source base, so it conservatively assumes it may be null. The destination is a local or field array, so it is unlikely to be null. There is no evidence of overlapping memory regions, so overlap is not a concern here. The main risk is dereferencing a potentially null source pointer, which can lead to a crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the source pointer to prevent undefined behavior if it is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_ptr_0[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(utmp_00, b_ptr_0[b].scales + 24 * sb, 12);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source pointer is null\n    // e.g., log an error, return an error code, or take other appropriate action\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory copy only occurs if the source pointer is valid, preventing a potential crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f32a8daa-2c10-3535-a31f-9d67648a46a6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array of four 32-bit unsigned integers, and the copy size is 12 bytes, which fits within the destination. However, dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which would also cause undefined behavior if dereferenced. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue. Developers should ensure that the source and destination do not overlap and that the source pointer is always valid before performing the copy.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, validate that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (utmp_1 != (b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24) && b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr) {\n    memmove(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n}\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add assertions to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(utmp_1 != (b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24));\nassert(b_ptr[b].scales != nullptr);\nmemcpy(utmp_1, b_ptr[b].scales + 12 + sb * 24, 12);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
